target:
Workers at high risk places:
(a) a hospital;
(b) a residential facility
(c) a custodial correctional facility within the meaning of section 11 of the Correctional Services Act 2014;
(d) a detention centre within the meaning of section 5 of the Youth Justice Act 2005;
(e) an aged care facility;
(f) a renal hostel;
(g) a family violence shelter;
(h) a sobering up shelter;
(i) a homeless shelter.

(a) a worker who, during the course of work, is likely to come into contact with a vulnerable person;
(b) a worker whose workplace poses a high risk of infection with COVID-19;
(c) a worker who performs work that is necessary for the operation or maintenance of essential infrastructure or essential logistics in the Territory.

Prohibited persons
a person who has been in a red zone in a State, Territory or Green Zone Country in the 14 days prior to entry to Victoria but does not include:
(a) a person described in clause 5(1)(b);
(b) a Victorian resident described in clause 6(1); or
(c) a Victorian resident described in clause 7(1);

exemptions:
Unvaccinated individuals may enter a community care services facility or accommodation to:
(a) perform a law enforcement function;
(b) fulfill a statutory duty under Commonwealth law;
(c) exercise rights under a Fair Work, Work Health and Safety, or Industrial Relations Act entry permit; or
(d) respond to an emergency when no vaccinated person is available.

Unvaccinated individuals may enter an airport or border crossing checkpoint to:
(a) perform a law enforcement function;
(b) fulfill a statutory duty under Commonwealth law;
(c) exercise rights under a Fair Work, Work Health and Safety, or Industrial Relations Act entry permit; or
(d) respond to an emergency when no vaccinated person is available.

Unvaccinated individuals may enter an airport or border crossing checkpoint to:
(a) perform a law enforcement function;
(b) fulfill a statutory duty under Commonwealth law;
(c) exercise rights under a Fair Work, Work Health and Safety, or Industrial Relations Act entry permit; or
(d) respond to an emergency when no vaccinated person is available.

Nothing in these directions prevents an unvaccinated person from entering a site for:
(a) performing a law enforcement function that requires site access;
(b) fulfilling a statutory duty under Commonwealth law that requires site access;
(c) exercising rights under a Fair Work, Work Health and Safety, or Industrial Relations Act entry permit; or
(d) responding to an emergency when no vaccinated person is available.

Unvaccinated individuals may enter a community care services facility or accommodation to:
(a) perform a law enforcement function;
(b) fulfill a statutory duty under Commonwealth law;
(c) exercise rights under a Fair Work, Work Health and Safety, or Industrial Relations Act entry permit; or
(d) respond to an emergency when no vaccinated person is available.

Nothing in these directions prevents an unvaccinated person from boarding an exposed or unexposed vessel, dedicated conveyance, or entering a port for the purpose of:
(a) performing a law enforcement function that requires boarding the vessel or entering the port;
(b) fulfilling a statutory duty under Commonwealth law that requires boarding the vessel or entering the port;
(c) exercising rights under a Fair Work or Work Health and Safety entry permit, or a permit under the Industrial Relations Act 1979 (WA); or
(d) responding to an emergency when no vaccinated or partially vaccinated person is available.

Unvaccinated individuals may drive or board commercial or public transport to:
(a) perform a law enforcement function (excluding security officers or authorized persons on public transport);
(b) fulfill a statutory duty under Commonwealth law;
(c) exercise rights under a Fair Work, Work Health and Safety, or Industrial Relations Act entry permit; or
(d) respond to an emergency when no vaccinated person is available.

Unvaccinated individuals may enter a critical business site to:
(a) perform a law enforcement function;
(b) fulfill a statutory duty under Commonwealth law;
(c) exercise rights under a Fair Work, Work Health and Safety, or Industrial Relations Act entry permit; or
(d) respond to an emergency when no vaccinated person is available

Unvaccinated remote Aboriginal community workers may enter or remain in a remote Aboriginal community for:
(a) performing a law enforcement function;
(b) family or cultural purposes, for as long as necessary;
(c) transiting through, without contact with residents and taking the most direct route;
(d) residing in the community without leaving;
(e) fulfilling a statutory duty under Commonwealth law;
(f) exercising rights under a Fair Work, Work Health and Safety, or Industrial Relations Act entry permit;
(g) responding to an emergency when no vaccinated person is available; or
(h) any circumstance approved in writing by the Chief Health Officer.

Unvaccinated individuals may enter a critical government regulator site to:
(a) perform a law enforcement function;
(b) fulfill a statutory duty under Commonwealth law;
(c) exercise rights under a Fair Work or Work Health and Safety entry permit; or
(d) respond to an emergency when no vaccinated person is available.

Unvaccinated individuals may enter a Ministerial site to:
(a) perform a law enforcement function;
(b) fulfill a statutory duty under Commonwealth law;
(c) exercise rights under a Fair Work, Work Health and Safety, or Industrial Relations Act entry permit;
(d) respond to an emergency when no vaccinated person is available; or
(e) under any written approval from the Chief Health Officer, subject to conditions.

